## 5. The strongest existing alternative

The closest comparator is Tobias–Breuckmann–Placke’s **“Optimal Decoding with the Worm”**. It already targets logical-class probabilities with noisy measurements, discusses the dimer representation, and gives a rigorous mixing bound in terms of *defect susceptibility*. Its efficient behavior for typical errors throughout the decodable phase is argued non-rigorously. Its Appendix D explicitly connects a uniform sampling capability to the formerly unresolved general perfect-matching approximation problem. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}

The distinction earned above is a uniform polynomial guarantee without an assumed susceptibility or mixing bound, together with calibration and relative-risk accounting. A certified sampler could also support near-Bayes decisions; I am not claiming that all sampling approaches inherently require inverse logical-error work.

Exact planar maximum-likelihood decoding is already available for important **noiseless-syndrome** models. Bravyi–Suchara–Vargo also provide tensor-network approximations for broader noise. The selected receiver’s additional difficulty is the nonplanar spacetime graph generated by repeated faulty measurements. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}

This is a constructive composition of existing results with the explicit reliability derivation above. I have not established historical priority for the composition.
